  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 42a7ef911f4c60e408989a759d2b9b18, type: 3}
  m_Name: SQL_WHERE
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::CodeEscape.Programming.LessonData
  id: SQL_WHERE
  language: 3
  conceptTitle: 'WHERE: Filtering Rows'
  symbol: WHERE
  relatedQuestionIds:
  - SQL_02_WHERE
  - HZ_SQL_02_WHERE
  topics:
  - WHERE
  definition: WHERE filters a query, so only the rows that match a condition are
    returned.
  howItWorks: WHERE comes after FROM table_name. Write a condition such as animal
    = 'Dog' or age > 3. Text values go inside single quotes; numbers don't need quotes.
    Rows that don't match the condition are left out.
  exampleCode: "Table: pets\n id | name  | animal | age\n  1 | Bolt  | Dog    |  
    4\n  2 | Misty | Cat    |   2\n  3 | Rex   | Dog    |   7\n\nSELECT name FROM
    pets\nWHERE animal = 'Dog';"
  exampleExplanation: The query checks the animal column of every row. Bolt and Rex
    are dogs, so the result is Bolt and Rex. Misty is a cat, so her row is left out.
  tip: The order is always SELECT ... FROM ... WHERE ...
